course: dictionary
lesson: 14536
title: Persistently: again and again
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Persistently is an adverb, so it tells you how someone does something. It means continuing again and again without stopping or giving up. «Mia persistently practised her piano scales until she could play them perfectly.» Mia did not stop or give up, so persistently means she kept going.
explain: Persistently can also mean annoyingly often. «Tom was persistently late for school.» Being late again and again is a problem, so here the word is a complaint. The words around persistently tell you which meaning the writer wants.
explain: Try the swap check. In the first sentence, "kept on trying" fits well. In the second, "late again and again" fits too, but the feeling is different: one word is praise and the other is a complaint.
fact: Persistently is an adverb, so it describes how an action is done.
fact: «Persistently» means continuing again and again without stopping or giving up.
fact: «Persistently» can also mean annoyingly often, as in «persistently late».
fact: In a school report, «worked persistently» is praise, but «persistently talking» is a complaint.
fact: In science, «a persistently high temperature» is one that stays high for a long time.
exercise: «Ava persistently tried the hard puzzle until she solved it.» What does persistently mean? | *Continuing again and again without giving up | Annoyingly, so everyone was cross | Only once | Quickly and without thinking
exercise: A teacher writes, «Sam persistently talks in class.» What kind of comment is this? | Praise for hard work | *A complaint | A science fact | A story about a dragon
exercise: «The dog barked persistently at the postman.» Find the adverb in this sentence. | dog | barked | *persistently | postman
test: «The doctor was worried because the fever stayed persistently high.» What does persistently suggest here? | Happy and bright | *Lasting a long time without going down | Very cold | Quickly gone
test: «She worked persistently on her project, and it was the best in the class.» Is this praise or a complaint? | Complaint | A warning about the weather | A rude word | *Praise
exam: Jayden says persistently is always a bad word. Is he right? | Yes, it only appears in science | *No, it can show praise or a complaint depending on the context | Yes, it always means annoying | No, it can mean continuing without giving up, which is praise
